## Batch-Level Background Consistency (`analyzeBackgroundScene` + `frozenSceneDescription`)

When the user uploads an inspiration image as the global background, the VTON pipeline now runs a single Gemini 3.1 Pro pre-pass at the *start* of every Generate batch. The pre-pass produces a deterministic structured "frozen scene description" that is reused VERBATIM across every per-pose `generateVTONPrompt` call in the batch. The mechanism makes a 50-pose batch read as 50 photographs taken in the same physical location — without object positions drifting left/right between framings, and without lighting drifting between poses.

### Pipeline integration

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Click[User clicks Generate] --> Has{background.mode == inspiration<br/>AND inspirationImage?}
    Has -->|Yes| Analyze["analyzeBackgroundScene()<br/>(Gemini 3.1 Pro, ONE call per unique inspiration image)"]
    Has -->|No| Skip[Skip]
    Analyze --> Frozen[frozenSceneDescription: locked WIDE-SHOT layout + hex palette + flat-light directive]
    Skip --> Frozen
    Frozen --> Loop[For each pose in batch]
    Loop --> Prompt["generateVTONPrompt() with frozenSceneDescription +<br/>FRAMING-AWARE SCENE PROJECTION rules"]
    Prompt --> Image["generateVTONImage()"]
```

In bulk mode, the pre-pass is run once per *unique* inspiration `File` referenced by any combination's effective background (default + per-pose overrides). The results are keyed by `File` reference into a `Map<File, string>` and looked up at `processResult` time.

### `analyzeBackgroundScene()` — output structure

The function emits a fixed-format multi-section text block (no preamble, no closing chatter). The downstream meta-prompter embeds this entire string verbatim:

```
# WIDE-SHOT LAYOUT
- <object_name>: x_center=<0.00–1.00>, width=<0.00–1.00>, vertical_zone=<sky | upper-third | upper-two-thirds | middle | middle-and-floor | floor | full-height>, depth=<foreground | midground | background>, description=<10–25 words, product-agnostic>

# COMPONENTS
<flat comma-separated list of every visible scene element, in product-agnostic language>

# PALETTE
- background-primary: #RRGGBB
- mid-tone: #RRGGBB
- accent: #RRGGBB
- highlight: #RRGGBB
- shadow: #RRGGBB

# MATERIALS & SURFACES
<2–4 sentences: ground texture, walls, atmospheric character, depth-of-field>

# SUBJECT FRAMING ANCHOR
"The model stands centered at x_center=0.5, occupying approximately the central 25–35% of the wide-shot frame, with both feet on the visible ground plane."

# LIGHTING (FLAT-OVERRIDE — MANDATORY VERBATIM SENTENCE)
"The model is lit by flat, evenly diffused light from a large overhead soft-source — a softbox-and-bounce setup at approximately 5500K daylight color temperature. The light wraps the model uniformly with minimal shadow falloff: cheeks, neck, collarbones, arms, hands, hips, and legs all read at the same exposure with no harsh side-shadow, no chiaroscuro, and no dramatic rim light. Background atmospheric lighting may follow the reference's overall mood, but light on the model itself stays flat and even."

# ATMOSPHERE & MOOD
<2–3 sentences>

# BACKGROUND LOCK CONTRACT
"This scene description is FROZEN for the entire batch. Every per-pose output prompt must reuse the WIDE-SHOT LAYOUT coordinates, the PALETTE hex codes, and the LIGHTING sentence VERBATIM, without paraphrasing, substituting synonyms, or drifting toward a default studio backdrop."
```

### Hard constraints baked into the analysis

1. **BRAND-BLIND** — never names brands or real-world buildings by proper name.
2. **PRODUCT-AGNOSTIC** — no assumption about what garment / footwear / model is later placed in the scene.
3. **FORCE-FLAT LIGHTING OVERRIDE** — *regardless* of how the source image is lit (harsh sun, golden-hour, deep shadows), the LIGHTING section emits the verbatim flat-lighting sentence. This satisfies the explicit user requirement: "the model is evenly illuminated with minimal shadows" no matter what the reference looks like.
4. **DETERMINISM** — coordinates and hex codes are exact; the description is reproducible.

### `generateVTONPrompt` background precedence (with `frozenSceneDescription`)

When `frozenSceneDescription` is passed in, the BACKGROUND section in the meta-prompt becomes:

1. per-pose `customPose.customBackground` (unchanged — pose-specific override always wins)
2. custom-pose IMAGE-REFERENCE mode (unchanged)
3. **NEW**: `frozenSceneDescription` if provided — embed verbatim, drop the inspiration-image inline part (no need to re-send the image because Gemini Pro already has the locked text)
4. `background.mode === "inspiration"` (legacy fallback when analysis was skipped or failed)
5. `background.textDescription` (unchanged)
6. default fallback (unchanged)

### `FRAMING-AWARE SCENE PROJECTION` directive (NEW)

When path (3) is active, `generateVTONPrompt` appends a `FRAMING-AWARE SCENE PROJECTION` directive to the meta-prompt. This is the actual mechanism that makes "tower on the left of the wide shot stays on the left of the close-up". It works because Gemini 3.1 Pro is the meta-prompter — it reads the wide-shot layout and emits a per-pose prompt whose BACKGROUND section honors the layout regardless of the framing's vertical crop.

```
═══ FRAMING-AWARE SCENE PROJECTION (MANDATORY when frozenSceneDescription is present) ═══
The WIDE-SHOT LAYOUT above describes the full scene as it appears in a head-to-toe full-body framing.
For the current pose with framing = {framing}, you MUST project that wide-shot scene through the
framing's crop window WITHOUT moving any object's normalized x_center.

Projection rules (NON-NEGOTIABLE):
1. HORIZONTAL ANCHORING: every object listed in WIDE-SHOT LAYOUT keeps its x_center exactly as
   given. Object positions NEVER drift left or right between framings.
2. VERTICAL VISIBILITY by framing:
   - full-body                → entire vertical_zone visible (sky → floor)
   - three-quarter            → upper-two-thirds + middle visible; floor cropped near the feet
   - mid-thigh                → upper-two-thirds + upper-middle visible; floor not visible
   - waist-up                 → upper-third + upper-two-thirds visible; floor + lower-middle cropped
   - bust-up / cropped headshot → ONLY upper-third + immediate background behind chest/head
   - hip-down / knee-down     → middle + floor visible; sky / upper-third cropped
   - product-only / feet-closeup → tightly-cropped sliver of floor only
   - ghost-mannequin          → background follows the FROZEN SCENE DESCRIPTION as for full-body
3. SCENE GEOMETRY LOCK: architectural elements keep the same vanishing-point geometry — a column
   to the model's left in the full-body shot is still to the model's left, at the same x_center.
4. PALETTE LOCK: copy the PALETTE hex codes verbatim into the BACKGROUND section.
5. ATMOSPHERE LOCK: depth-of-field, materials, atmospheric character identical to the wide-shot.
6. FLAT LIGHTING LOCK: copy the LIGHTING (FLAT-OVERRIDE) sentence verbatim. No chiaroscuro,
   no harsh side-shadows, no dramatic rim lights.
```

### Cost & telemetry

- One extra `gemini-3.1-pro-preview` call (`ThinkingLevel.MEDIUM`) per Generate batch (or per unique inspiration image in bulk mode).
- The cost row shows up under `Background Scene Analysis (Gemini 3.1 Pro)`.

